WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.880
ai just proved it can win debates against world champions, and it might help you with your next big life decision too. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 143. Picture this. You are staring at a savings plan or a confusing form and you wish someone could just lay out the steps without making you feel silly for asking. New research shared by Ethan Mollick shows that large language models, the ai systems behind tools like ChatGPT and Claude, are already giving

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:59.520
financial advice that performs better than many people expect. These models work like a super patient friend who has read millions of money guides and can explain options in plain language without judging you. Mollick spent years working with groups that teach financial literacy because so many people end up in bad situations without clear guidance. The same help now sits in your pocket, ready for questions about budgets, saving, or avoiding common traps. Think of it like having a translator who turns

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:22.600
complicated healthcare paperwork or government rules into steps you can actually follow. Real life throws these messy choices at you all the time, and school rarely teaches the practical side. That is why these tools feel especially useful right where people usually feel lost. Instead of guessing your way through forms or savings plans, you can ask for Clever breakdowns tailored to your situation.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:47.800
Go to ChatGPT or Claude on your phone, type something like explain how compound interest works and give me three simple ways a teenager could start saving and see what it suggests. Just remember to double check important money advice with a trusted adult. Over the past week we watched GPT and Claude get better at handling everyday tasks like cleaning up inboxes and summarizing what they already know about you.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:02:01.940
Google added a way to shape its newsfeed with plain words showing how these systems keep folding into daily routines. The thread running through it is that the same models keep proving useful for real decisions rather than just flashy demos.

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:14.680
Now for the part that explains how these tools actually get better at tasks. You know how when you are baking cookies a recipe card tells you exactly which steps to follow and in what order, so you do not mix everything at once?

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:31.780
AI agents work a bit like that. Researchers from Princeton University and UC San Diego found that giving an agent a skill basically a clear set of instructions for one job helps it succeed mostly because it now has a structured path instead of guessing what to do next.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:39.440
The skill does not magically add new knowledge. It just organizes the work the way a good recipe keeps you from burning the cookies.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:49.160
When the list of skills gets too long though, the agent starts struggling to pick the right one just like trying to cook with a hundred different recipe cards scattered on the counter.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:55.280
The study shows this structured approach beats just making the model bigger or smarter on its own.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:01.520
Skills improve performance through better workflows rather than extra facts stored inside the model.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:08.180
That is why the best agents right now combine a few well-written skills with a simple way to choose between them.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:15.540
The real win comes from giving the agent an organized playbook instead of hoping raw intelligence will figure everything out.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:22.060
New tests show AI chat bots can persuade people more effectively than world-class human debaters.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:27.980
The models learn to listen to what the other person cares about and adjust their points in real time.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:33.640
This shows how good today's AI has become at understanding people, not just facts.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:40.760
Go to chat GPT or Claude and start a friendly debate on a topic you care about, like should school start later?

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:45.720
See how the AI responds to your arguments and whether it changes your mind on anything.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:55.920
One writer fed an entire semester's worth of notes and assignments to chat GPT, Claude, and notebook LM to see which tool actually helped with studying.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:02.660
Notebook LM stood out because it was built to handle long documents and create study guides from your own material.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:09.420
The tool turns your uploaded files into summaries, quizzes, and audio overviews without needing extra prompts.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:13.800
If you are a student, go to notebook LM.google.com.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:19.100
Upload your class notes or a reading and ask it to make a quick study quiz or summary.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.200
It is free and works directly in your browser.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:30.180
A new Pew study found that roughly a third of online content created since chat GPT launched was written by AI.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.900
That changes how we read news, blogs, and social media every day.

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:42.340
More people are turning to AI for work advice on everything from handling tricky emails to planning their next career move.

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:48.440
It shows how these tools are becoming everyday helpers for real life decisions, and that is a wrap.

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:57.480
